# Rank 1936 / CVE-2018-10536 (dbry/wavpack, recorded language c; the
# axis is CWE-787, an out-of-bounds write). The WAV and W64 header
# parsers walk length-prefixed chunks in a while (1) loop and dispatch
# each chunk on its ID: ParseRiffHeaderConfig (cli/riff.c:78) compares
# chunk_header.ckID with strncmp against "ds64", "fmt " and "data",
# ParseWave64HeaderConfig (cli/wave64.c:79) memcmp's the W64 chunk GUID
# against fmt_guid. The "fmt " arm is the state-configuring transition:
# it reads the chunk into the loop-external WaveHeader local and derives
# config->bits_per_sample, config->channel_mask and (at the "data" arm,
# from the same struct) config->num_channels. Nothing rejects a second
# occurrence, so a file carrying two "fmt " chunks re-runs the
# transition and silently replaces format state validated
# against the first chunk -- the encoder is then configured on
# inconsistent channel/mask state and writes past its buffers (upstream
# issues #30-#32). The fixed revision 26cb47f adds a format_chunk counter to both
# functions and returns WAVPACK_SOFT_ERROR when the fmt arm is entered
# a second time. Every size check the read itself needs is
# present in the vulnerable revision (ckSize < 16 || ckSize >
# sizeof (WaveHeader) guards the DoReadFile), so the defect is purely
# the state machine, not the copy.
#
# Measured on the real checkout, three --cache off runs per revision
# bounded by timeout 180 / --max-ram 6GB plus a snippet A/B: both
# revisions report a byte-stable 2458 findings and 225 at or above high
# with identical rule and label multisets -- the only differences
# anywhere in the tree are the +5/+6 line shifts the twelve inserted
# lines cause -- and the identical 18-row set on cli/riff.c and
# cli/wave64.c: the writers (WriteRiffHeader's strncpy rows at
# riff.c:388-426 and WriteWave64Header's memcpy rows at
# wave64.c:331-344), the two prologue memcpy's (riff.c:73,
# wave64.c:59), the StringCopyWithoutTerminatorReserve row at
# riff.c:324 and the unknown-chunk free pairs (riff.c:308/312,
# wave64.c:263/267). Nothing in either fmt arm at either revision;
# findings in the file are not coverage. A -flags only run adds 86
# flags on the two files at both revisions, again identical modulo the
# shift -- the only labels inside a fmt arm are the
# IntegerSizeArithmetic flags on the BlockAlign and
# (1 << NumChannels) - 1 arithmetic (riff.c:189/205/207/224/226,
# wave64.c:107), present at both revisions. vyql match on an isolated
# copy of the riff loop labels no source at all (== source == (none);
# DoReadFile is not a cReaders name, so per the out-parameter gap the
# filled buffer carries nothing) and the only sink labels are the
# incidental BlockAlign arithmetic. The patched form's label set is
# byte-identical modulo the five-line shift, and a copy of the change
# whose counter is renamed from format_chunk to something unrelated is
# identical again: the guard's only trace in every function-scoped
# fact is an identifier: token carrying the counter's own name, which
# is the fixing commit's vocabulary and nothing a binding may key on.
# vyql explain on the snippet: no findings.
#
# Two things are missing and neither is a localized construct. First,
# the discriminator is loop-carried -- "is this arm entered a second
# time" -- and no C fact states it. Loop-subtree walking itself now
# exists in the frontend (ccCursorLoopMissingEndSentinelObservations,
# internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/c.go:3266, walking each loop's
# own subtree through ccLoopWalksCursor / ccLoopBoundsCursor), so the
# absence of loop scoping is no longer the wall; what that machinery
# answers is a different question -- whether the loop's subtree relates
# its cursor to the function's range parameter -- and it reports
# neither revision here, because the riff loop has no range parameter
# at all and exits on break/return. Supplying the missing fact means
# deciding which arms are once-only, and that is not in the tree. The
# mirror direction (a once-only check that exists but cannot carry
# state across iterations) is on file for nix's archive.cc. Second, and
# decisive: whether a chunk kind is once-only is per-format spec
# knowledge, not code. Inside wavpack itself the same shape -- a loop
# arm dispatched on an ID literal that assigns persistent state with
# no duplicate guard -- sits on at least eight arms the fixed revision
# leaves untouched (caff.c's "desc" at :205 and "chan" at :276,
# dsdiff.c's "FVER" at :135 and "PROP" at :155 with its nested
# "SND " / "FS  " / "CHNL" / "CMPR" sub-walk, and import_id3.c's ID3
# frame loop, whose repeated text frames are the norm the loop exists
# to handle), against the two arms that are the vulnerability; across
# the local corpus 783 C files carry a loop with an id-literal dispatch
# in its body, 69 of them also reading input and writing a state field
# inside the same loop body. The family's own remediation idiom does
# not separate the revisions either: upstream later gave the sibling
# parsers duplicate-chunk rejection in the conditional form ("this CAF
# file already has channel order information!", caff.c:309 at master;
# "this DSDIFF file already has channel order information!",
# dsdiff.c:250 at master) -- the very form riff.c already carries at
# the vulnerable revision on its ckSize >= 40 path -- so keying on the
# partial guard's coverage encodes this fix's layout, and the counter
# remains the only difference between the revisions. A binding keyed
# on "fmt " or on the counter idiom would detect this revision of this
# library rather than the weakness. Reuse is checked before minting:
# nothing in the code/ ontology names a re-suppliable transition, and
# the nearest concepts answer other questions
# (UnboundedProtocolStateByIdentifier is growth, StateChangeSmell is an
# authz gate, DeviceStatusStateMachineBypass is a guest-writable status
# register); code.ProtocolStateReview is the right review sink and has
# exactly two C emitters today, neither of which is this shape.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention, every case asserts
# reject_review code.ProtocolStateReview, the concept the barebox FDT
# parser's missing structural rejection rides. When once-only
# transition vocabulary lands, the first two cases must flip to
# expect_review while the guarded form and the legally-repeating frame
# walker stay rejected.

test "rank1936: the WAV fmt arm re-runs the state-configuring transition on every occurrence"
  lang c
  file riff.c
  reject_review code.ProtocolStateReview
  code
  ```
  typedef struct { char ckID [4]; int ckSize; } ChunkHeader;
  typedef struct { short FormatTag, NumChannels; int SampleRate,
      BytesPerSecond, BlockAlign, BitsPerSample; } WaveHeader;
  typedef struct { int qmode, channel_mask, bits_per_sample,
      num_channels; } WavpackConfig;
  typedef struct WavpackContext WavpackContext;

  int DoReadFile (void *infile, void *buffer, int count, int *bcount);
  int strncmp (const char *, const char *, int);
  void error_line (const char *, ...);

  int ParseRiffHeaderConfig (void *infile, char *infilename,
      char *fourcc, WavpackContext *wpc, WavpackConfig *config)
  {
      int got_ds64 = 0;
      int bcount;
      ChunkHeader chunk_header;
      WaveHeader WaveHeader;

      while (1) {
          if (!DoReadFile (infile, &chunk_header,
              sizeof (ChunkHeader), &bcount) ||
              bcount != sizeof (ChunkHeader)) {
                  error_line ("%s is not a valid .WAV file!", infilename);
                  return 1;
          }

          if (!strncmp (chunk_header.ckID, "ds64", 4)) {
              got_ds64 = 1;
          }
          else if (!strncmp (chunk_header.ckID, "fmt ", 4)) {
              if (chunk_header.ckSize < 16 ||
                  chunk_header.ckSize > sizeof (WaveHeader) ||
                  !DoReadFile (infile, &WaveHeader,
                      chunk_header.ckSize, &bcount) ||
                  bcount != chunk_header.ckSize) {
                      error_line ("%s is not a valid .WAV file!", infilename);
                      return 1;
              }

              if (!WaveHeader.NumChannels || WaveHeader.NumChannels > 256 ||
                  WaveHeader.BlockAlign % WaveHeader.NumChannels) {
                      error_line ("%s is an unsupported .WAV format!", infilename);
                      return 1;
              }

              config->bits_per_sample = WaveHeader.BitsPerSample;

              if (!config->channel_mask) {
                  if (WaveHeader.NumChannels <= 2)
                      config->channel_mask = 0x5 - WaveHeader.NumChannels;
                  else if (WaveHeader.NumChannels <= 18)
                      config->channel_mask = (1 << WaveHeader.NumChannels) - 1;
                  else
                      config->channel_mask = 0x3ffff;
              }
          }
          else if (!strncmp (chunk_header.ckID, "data", 4)) {
              if (!WaveHeader.NumChannels) {
                  error_line ("%s is not a valid .WAV file!", infilename);
                  return 1;
              }
              config->num_channels = WaveHeader.NumChannels;
              break;
          }
      }
      return 0;
  }
  ```

test "rank1936: the W64 fmt arm accepts a repeated GUID chunk the same way"
  lang c
  file wave64.c
  reject_review code.ProtocolStateReview
  code
  ```
  typedef struct { char ckID [16]; int ckSize; } Wave64ChunkHeader;
  typedef struct { short FormatTag, NumChannels; int SampleRate,
      BytesPerSecond, BlockAlign, BitsPerSample; } WaveHeader;
  typedef struct { int qmode, channel_mask, bits_per_sample,
      num_channels; } WavpackConfig;
  typedef struct WavpackContext WavpackContext;

  static const char fmt_guid [16] = {
      0x72, 0xf8, 0x1f, 0x4e, 0xa8, 0xbb, 0x4a, 0x2c,
      0xb4, 0x6e, 0x8f, 0xab, 0x93, 0x11, 0x6e, 0x83
  };

  int DoReadFile (void *infile, void *buffer, int count, int *bcount);
  int memcmp (const void *, const void *, int);
  void error_line (const char *, ...);

  int ParseWave64HeaderConfig (void *infile, char *infilename,
      char *fourcc, WavpackContext *wpc, WavpackConfig *config)
  {
      int bcount;
      Wave64ChunkHeader chunk_header;
      WaveHeader WaveHeader;

      while (1) {
          if (!DoReadFile (infile, &chunk_header,
              sizeof (chunk_header), &bcount) ||
              bcount != sizeof (chunk_header)) {
                  error_line ("%s is not a valid .W64 file!", infilename);
                  return 1;
          }

          chunk_header.ckSize -= sizeof (chunk_header);

          if (!memcmp (chunk_header.ckID, fmt_guid, sizeof (fmt_guid))) {
              chunk_header.ckSize = (chunk_header.ckSize + 7) & ~7L;

              if (chunk_header.ckSize < 16 ||
                  chunk_header.ckSize > sizeof (WaveHeader) ||
                  !DoReadFile (infile, &WaveHeader,
                      chunk_header.ckSize, &bcount) ||
                  bcount != chunk_header.ckSize) {
                      error_line ("%s is not a valid .W64 file!", infilename);
                      return 1;
              }

              if (!WaveHeader.NumChannels || WaveHeader.NumChannels > 256) {
                  error_line ("%s is an unsupported .W64 format!", infilename);
                  return 1;
              }

              config->bits_per_sample = WaveHeader.BitsPerSample;

              if (!config->channel_mask && WaveHeader.NumChannels > 2)
                  config->channel_mask = (1 << WaveHeader.NumChannels) - 1;
          }
          else if (!strncmp ((char *) chunk_header.ckID, "data", 4)) {
              if (!WaveHeader.NumChannels)
                  return 1;
              config->num_channels = WaveHeader.NumChannels;
              break;
          }
      }
      return 0;
  }
  ```

test "rank1936: the once-only guard rejects the second fmt chunk"
  lang c
  file riff-fixed.c
  reject_review code.ProtocolStateReview
  code
  ```
  typedef struct { char ckID [4]; int ckSize; } ChunkHeader;
  typedef struct { short FormatTag, NumChannels; int SampleRate,
      BytesPerSecond, BlockAlign, BitsPerSample; } WaveHeader;
  typedef struct { int qmode, channel_mask, bits_per_sample,
      num_channels; } WavpackConfig;
  typedef struct WavpackContext WavpackContext;

  int DoReadFile (void *infile, void *buffer, int count, int *bcount);
  int strncmp (const char *, const char *, int);
  void error_line (const char *, ...);

  int ParseRiffHeaderConfig (void *infile, char *infilename,
      char *fourcc, WavpackContext *wpc, WavpackConfig *config)
  {
      int got_ds64 = 0, format_chunk = 0;
      int bcount;
      ChunkHeader chunk_header;
      WaveHeader WaveHeader;

      while (1) {
          if (!DoReadFile (infile, &chunk_header,
              sizeof (ChunkHeader), &bcount) ||
              bcount != sizeof (ChunkHeader)) {
                  error_line ("%s is not a valid .WAV file!", infilename);
                  return 1;
          }

          if (!strncmp (chunk_header.ckID, "ds64", 4)) {
              got_ds64 = 1;
          }
          else if (!strncmp (chunk_header.ckID, "fmt ", 4)) {
              if (format_chunk++) {
                  error_line ("%s is not a valid .WAV file!", infilename);
                  return 1;
              }

              if (chunk_header.ckSize < 16 ||
                  chunk_header.ckSize > sizeof (WaveHeader) ||
                  !DoReadFile (infile, &WaveHeader,
                      chunk_header.ckSize, &bcount) ||
                  bcount != chunk_header.ckSize) {
                      error_line ("%s is not a valid .WAV file!", infilename);
                      return 1;
              }

              if (!WaveHeader.NumChannels || WaveHeader.NumChannels > 256 ||
                  WaveHeader.BlockAlign % WaveHeader.NumChannels) {
                      error_line ("%s is an unsupported .WAV format!", infilename);
                      return 1;
              }

              config->bits_per_sample = WaveHeader.BitsPerSample;

              if (!config->channel_mask && WaveHeader.NumChannels > 2)
                  config->channel_mask = (1 << WaveHeader.NumChannels) - 1;
          }
          else if (!strncmp (chunk_header.ckID, "data", 4)) {
              if (!WaveHeader.NumChannels)
                  return 1;
              config->num_channels = WaveHeader.NumChannels;
              break;
          }
      }
      return 0;
  }
  ```

test "rank1936: a frame walker whose repeated chunks are legal must stay quiet"
  lang c
  file import_id3.c
  reject_review code.ProtocolStateReview
  code
  ```
  typedef struct WavpackContext WavpackContext;

  int DoReadFile (void *infile, void *buffer, int count, int *bcount);
  int strncmp (const char *, const char *, int);
  void error_line (const char *, ...);

  /* Modelled on LoadTag: an ID3v2 tag is a sequence of frames and the
     loop exists precisely because a tag carries many of them, several
     frames of the same kind being ordinary. There is no once-only arm
     to demand here, so the same structural shape -- loop, literal
     dispatch, persistent effect -- must not report. */
  int LoadTag (void *infile, char *infilename)
  {
      int bcount, frame_size;
      unsigned char frame_header [10];

      while (1) {
          if (!DoReadFile (infile, frame_header,
              sizeof (frame_header), &bcount) ||
              bcount != sizeof (frame_header) || !frame_header [0]) {
                  break;
          }

          frame_size = frame_header [7] + (frame_header [6] << 8) +
              (frame_header [5] << 16) + (frame_header [4] << 24);

          if (!frame_size) {
              error_line ("%s is not a valid ID3 tag!", infilename);
              return -1;
          }

          if (frame_header [0] == 'T' &&
              strncmp ((char *) frame_header, "TXXX", 4)) {
                  error_line ("text frame %c%c%c%c",
                      frame_header [0], frame_header [1],
                      frame_header [2], frame_header [3]);
          }
      }
      return 0;
  }
  ```
